  "content": {
    "id": "p31-stem83-86-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Solve the following for $0\\le x\\le 2\\pi$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p31-q83",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$2\\cos^2x=1-\\sin x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p31-q83-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{7\\pi}{6},\\frac{11\\pi}{6}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p31-q83-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\cos^2x=1-\\sin^2x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and factor the quadratic in ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sin x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Use $\\cos^2x=1-\\sin^2x$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2(1-\\sin^2x)&=1-\\sin x\\\\2\\sin^2x-\\sin x-1&=0\\\\(2\\sin x+1)(\\sin x-1)&=0\\\\\\sin x&=-\\frac12\\text{ or }1\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $\\sin x=-\\frac12$, the related angle is $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and sine is negative in quadrants 3 and 4. For $\\sin x=1$, the unit circle gives $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\n$$x=\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{7\\pi}{6},\\frac{11\\pi}{6}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 90
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q84",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\cos^2x=\\sin x-1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
          "worked": "Use $\\cos^2x=1-\\sin^2x$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}1-\\sin^2x&=\\sin x-1\\\\\\sin^2x+\\sin x-2&=0\\\\(\\sin x+2)(\\sin x-1)&=0\\\\\\sin x&=-2\\text{ or }1\\end{align*}$$\n\nReject $\\sin x=-2$ because $-1\\le\\sin x\\le1$. The unit circle gives\n\n$$x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q85",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\tan^2x-\\sec x-1=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p31-q85-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=\\frac{\\pi}{3},\\pi,\\frac{5\\pi}{3}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p31-q85-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x-1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", factor in ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sec x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", then solve for cosine.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The original equation requires $\\cos x\\ne0$. Use $\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x-1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sec^2x-1-\\sec x-1&=0\\\\\\sec^2x-\\sec x-2&=0\\\\(\\sec x-2)(\\sec x+1)&=0\\\\\\sec x&=2\\text{ or }-1\\\\\\cos x&=\\frac12\\text{ or }-1\\\\x&=\\frac{\\pi}{3},\\pi,\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\end{align*}$$\n\nAll three angles have nonzero cosine and satisfy the original equation.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 86
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q86",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\sec^2x=4\\tan x-2$.\n\nGive non-exact values correct to 2 decimal places.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p31-q86-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\frac{5\\pi}{4}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " or ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x\\approx1.25,4.39",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " radians.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p31-q86-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sec^2x=1+\\tan^2x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " to obtain ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "(\\tan x-1)(\\tan x-3)=0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The original equation requires $\\cos x\\ne0$. Use $\\sec^2x=1+\\tan^2x$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}1+\\tan^2x&=4\\tan x-2\\\\\\tan^2x-4\\tan x+3&=0\\\\(\\tan x-1)(\\tan x-3)&=0\\\\\\tan x&=1\\text{ or }3\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $\\tan x=1$,\n\n$$x=\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\frac{5\\pi}{4}.$$\n\nFor $\\tan x=3$, retain the full calculator value and add $\\pi$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\alpha&=\\tan^{-1}(3)\\\\x&=\\alpha\\text{ or }\\alpha+\\pi\\\\x&\\approx1.25\\text{ or }4.39\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe four solutions lie in the domain and have nonzero cosine.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 124
